course: arts
lesson: 12
title: Drawing: lines, shapes and drawing what you really see
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Drawing is a skill learned by practice, like riding a bike: almost every artist's early drawings look odd, and simple tricks help a lot.
explain: The biggest trick is to draw what you SEE, not what you THINK things look like: your brain has ready-made symbols, like a lollipop tree, but a real tree has messy branches, gaps and many greens.
explain: Artists start with light, simple shapes, circles, ovals and boxes, then add details on top; they show distance by making far things smaller (perspective) and show roundness by shading the side away from the light.
explain: Picture Ling drawing a horse whose legs look wrong: she checks a photo and sees that the joint that bends "backwards" is really the horse's ankle, because horses walk on tiptoe.
explain: Gen Z "mid" means average, "it's giving cartoon" means it looks like a cartoon, and "lowkey" means kind of; in Britain "binning" something means throwing it in the bin.
fact: Drawing improves a lot with practice; teachers agree that people are not simply born able or unable to draw, and keeping old drawings shows how far you have come.
fact: Artists lightly sketch big simple shapes first, then add details; light guide lines drawn first and rubbed out later are called construction lines.
fact: A reference is a photo or object you look at while you draw, like the photo Ling checked for her horse.
fact: Proportion means how big each part is compared with the others.
fact: On a real head the eyes sit about halfway down, not near the top.
fact: Drawing from life means drawing while looking at the real thing, noticing the actual shapes, gaps and shadows instead of a remembered symbol.
fact: Perspective shows distance on flat paper: far things look smaller, and parallel lines like road edges seem to meet at a vanishing point on the horizon.
fact: Italian artists worked out the rules of perspective about 600 years ago.
fact: Shading makes parts darker where light does not reach; a light side, a dark side and a shadow make a round object look 3D, and the brightest spot is the highlight.
fact: Most people will not notice one wobbly line, and you can draw over it; sketchbooks are full of wobbly lines.
fact: Anime and manga have their own style with big eyes and simple noses; copying a style is good practice, and drawing from life teaches different things.
fact: What looks like a horse's backward knee is really its ankle; its knee is higher up, and artists study anatomy so legs look right.
fact: Small shakes are normal; artists draw lightly with many strokes from the shoulder and elbow, then darken the line they like.
exercise: Kai's dog looks like a sausage and he says he is "mid" at drawing. What is true? | Drawing is a skill that improves a lot with practice | Some people are born unable ever to draw, however hard they try | One bad drawing proves he has no talent | Only children who go to art school can ever learn to draw
exercise: Josh says starting a cat with circles and triangles is cheating. What is true? | Starting with simple shapes is a standard artist method | Real artists draw everything perfectly in one go, with no shapes | Shapes are only allowed in maths, not art | Drawing in shapes always makes the cat look much worse at the end
exercise: What are construction lines? | Light guide lines drawn first and rubbed out later | Thick black outlines drawn at the very end of a drawing | Lines that show where a new building goes | The little measuring lines printed along a ruler
exercise: Priya puts the eyes right at the top of the head. Where do they really sit? | About halfway down the head | Right at the top, near the hair | Very low down, just above the chin | In the exact centre of the forehead
exercise: Oisín keeps drawing a lollipop tree. What should he do? | Look at the real tree and draw its actual shapes | Draw a lollipop, because that is just what trees are | Copy the tree from his old baby book again and again | Draw a brown stick with a green ball
exercise: Sophie's road looks flat. What makes it look deep? | The sides of the road meeting at a point in the distance | Drawing the far end the same width as the near end of the road | Colouring the whole road in bright red | Making the road wider and wider as it goes away into the distance
exercise: Who worked out the rules of perspective about 600 years ago? | Italian artists | Japanese printmakers | Ancient Egyptian builders | Cartoonists
exercise: Ahmed's apple is flat grey. What should he do? | Find where the light comes from and shade the other side | Colour the whole apple in one darker grey, all over | Draw a much thicker outline all the way around it | Shade it in random patches wherever he likes, with no light in mind
exercise: What is a highlight? | The brightest spot where light hits | The darkest shadow under an object | A thick outline drawn round a picture | The title at the top
exercise: Zoe makes one wobbly pen line and wants to bin the drawing. What is wise? | Keep going, most people won't notice and she can draw over it | Bin it, because one wrong line ruins any drawing for ever | Never use a pen again in her life | Start again from the beginning every single time a line wobbles
exercise: Jake teases that his mate only draws anime. What is fair? | Anime is a real skill, and drawing from life teaches other things | Anime drawing is not real art, so he should stop it and draw fruit | Only drawing real people counts as art | Big eyes are a mistake in every style of drawing ever made, including anime
exercise: Ling's horse's legs look wrong. What is the truth about horse legs? | The "backward knee" is really the ankle | Horses' knees really do bend backwards | Horses have no knees at all on their legs | Horses have the same legs as people
exercise: Ben says his hands are "too shaky" to draw. What do artists do? | Draw lightly with many strokes, then darken the line they like | Give up, because steady hands are needed for every kind of drawing | Only draw with a ruler for every line | Press as hard as possible with the pencil so the hand stops shaking
exercise: What is a reference when drawing? | A photo or object you look at while drawing | A teacher who marks your work at the end | A rubber that removes mistakes | A sketch you draw only from memory, without looking
exercise: What does proportion mean? | How big each part is compared with the others | How dark a shadow is under an object | How fast you draw a line | How many different colours you use in one picture
test: Your mate goes, "real artists never rub anything out, they just get it right first go." Is he right? | No, most start with rough shapes and fix them step by step | Yes, rubbers are banned in all art schools and studios | Yes, but only for painters | No, because real artists never actually draw anything at all
test: What would happen if Sophie drew the far end of the road just as wide as the near end? | The road would look flat instead of going into the distance | The road would look even deeper | The road would look like it is going uphill on purpose | Nothing would change
test: Oisín's grandad says, "Does it look like a lolly to ya?" What lesson is he teaching? | Draw what is in front of you, not what you remember | Trees should always be drawn as lollies | Real trees are all round and smooth, just like lollies | Drawing trees is too hard for children, so they should not try
test: A cartoon horse has knees that bend the same way as a person's. What would a real horse show? | The lower joint that bends back is the ankle, with the knee higher up | Horse legs bend exactly like ours do | Horses have four separate knees on each leg, all bending forwards | Horses have no joints at all in their legs, so they never bend them
test: Jake says his mate's drawing is "giving cartoon" (looks like a cartoon). What is the best advice for the mate? | Practise both his favourite style and real things | Stop drawing anime for ever and never go back | Only ever draw cartoons | Draw with his eyes closed so it looks more real
test: Your little sister draws a ball with no shading and it looks like a flat circle. What would make it look round? | A light side, a dark side and a shadow on the table | A thicker black outline all the way around | Colouring it all one dark colour | Drawing a big smiley face right in the middle of it
test: Zoe yells she's "binning it" (throwing it in the bin) over one wobble. What is the truth? | One wobbly line does not ruin a drawing | Every drawing with a wobble is ruined for good | Bins are where good art belongs | Pen drawings can never be fixed once a line goes wrong
test: Kai's brother says, "Some people can draw and some can't, end of." What does the lesson say? | Drawing improves a lot with practice | He is right, it is fixed from birth for everyone | Only left-handed people can draw | Drawing is pure luck every single time
test: Ben's aunt says her hands shake too. What does that show? | You don't need perfectly steady hands to draw | Only people with steady hands become illustrators | She must have stopped drawing | Shaky hands always make better artists than steady ones
test: Why keep your old bad drawings? | They show how far you have come | They are worth lots of money one day | Teachers want to mark them again | They can all be rubbed out later
test: Priya measures eyes on a photo. Where will she find them? | About halfway down the head | At the very top of the head, by the hair | Just under the nose | Exactly at the chin, near the mouth
exam: Identify the principal means by which perspective conveys distance upon a flat surface. | The diminution of distant objects and the convergence of parallel lines upon a vanishing point | The representation of all objects at an identical size, whatever their distance from the viewer | The use of bright colours for every distant object | The enlargement of objects in proportion to their distance from the eye of the observer
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the acquisition of skill in drawing. | Skill in drawing improves substantially with practice | Skill in drawing is fixed at birth and cannot be improved | Skill in drawing depends wholly upon the steadiness of the hand | Skill in drawing may be acquired solely at an academy of art
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the joint commonly mistaken for a backward-bending knee in the horse? | It is the ankle, the knee being situated higher upon the limb | It is a knee which bends contrary to that of a human | It is a joint peculiar to horses kept in captivity | It is an error in the drawing having no counterpart in nature
exam: Ascertain the position of the eyes upon a typical human head. | Approximately halfway between the crown and the chin | Immediately beneath the crown of the head | Immediately above the chin | Upon the uppermost quarter of the forehead
